    <div class="article-copy">
      <p>
        An event is more than the time spent in front of a stage. It is a
        journey made up of invitations, arrivals, conversations, content and the
        moments that follow.
      </p>
      <h2>Walk through the whole journey.</h2>
      <p>
        Looking at the experience from an attendee’s perspective can reveal
        questions that a floor plan alone cannot answer. Where will people go
        first? What do they need to find? How will they know what is happening
        next?
      </p>
      <p>
        Mapping that journey helps connect the physical environment with the
        information and support people need.
      </p>
      <div class="editorial-pullquote">
        <span class="cue" style="--gel: #90c74f">A useful question</span>What
        does someone need at this moment to feel welcome, informed and involved?
      </div>
      <h2>Consider different ways to take part.</h2>
      <p>
        People arrive with different needs and preferences. Clear wayfinding,
        accessible routes, readable content and places to step away can all be
        considered as part of the design process.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Make essential information easy to find before arrival.</li>
        <li>Review the route between key spaces.</li>
        <li>Balance focused sessions with opportunities to connect.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Give each moment a purpose.</h2>
      <p>
        An immersive experience does not need every moment to be busy. A
        thoughtful rhythm can give audiences time to listen, reflect, explore
        and contribute.
      </p>
      <p>
        Starting with the audience keeps the design focused on what the
        experience should help them feel, understand and remember.
      </p>
